te one write it once and never under a header that names it", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Headers, read in code: charts.py _BarsFields.table uses 'Group' / 'Row' / 'Value' / 'Parts' / `_interval_head(level)` ('95% interval ...') / 'Note'. ForestChart.table uses 'Group' / 'Row' / 'Change' / interval head / 'Note', and _DotIntervalFields.table uses 'Row' / 'Value' / interval head / 'Note'. The scorecard fold uses 'Value' / 'Interval' (scorecard.py:1685-1687). None names a unit. Cells: the value cell is `_unit_after(value_text, unit)` ('+10 pts', '85%'). The interval cell is `_interval(lo, hi, unit)`, with the unit once after the high end ('-29 to +58 pts', '80 to 89%'). Beside a figure (tooltips via `_PrintedInterval.beside`, glyph titles via `_scale_interval`, the line chart's 'value (lo to hi)'), the ends are bare. The moved goldens under tests/fixtures/hot_path_oracle are byte and sha256 pins only, so no cell text can be read there. The textual pins are test_number_policy_every_table (`['a', '+10 pts', '-29 to +58 pts']`, `['a', '85%', '80 to 89%']`) and test_number_policy_written_page ('-8 pts | -13 to -2 pts'). Borderline but within rule 3 as written: a row's label or note restates its figure with the unit ('margin -15 pts; shaded: ... | -15 pts', '-8 pts | ... | -8 pts worse'). Those are a label and a note, not a header. The one place where the unit is not written at all is the BLOCKING diverging finding above.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/charts.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/campaign/scorecard.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "Q5 answer: every moved pin encodes a ruling. None is loosened and none is deleted. All stay exact-match", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Each moved test, with the ruling it encodes. (1) test_number_policy_written_page, three asserts ('-8 pts', '-13 to -2 pts'; '-3 pts', '-7 to +3 pts'; the margin's value '-15 pts'): rule 3 (an interval standing alone ends on its unit; residual 5), and for the margin rule 4 (a declared value as declared, '-15 pts'). (2) test_presentation_figure_forms ('+16.7 pts, interval -7.8 to +35.4, margin \u00b15 pts'): residual 6 (a change's sign on the glyph title) plus rule 3. (3) test_presentation_number_forms ('-5.0 pts, interval -12.8 to +10.0'): rule 3. (4) test_report_charts (ends ('40', '60'), the table '40 to 60%' unchanged): rule 3. (5) test_report_golden_g2 SCOREBOARD_HEADER ('Cost per 1,000 replies'): the owner's C5b set-scope ruling (one form per measure across every page) and residual 9's money label; the re-pin carries its reason in a comment. (6) test_report_kit_contract, renamed to ..._carry_no_unit_... (('98%', '96', '100'), money unchanged): rule 3 plus the C5b 'Kit joins' ruling; the rename follows the new contract and loosens nothing. (7) test_report_svg, two titles: rule 3. (8) test_writer_eval_preference (('70%', '33', '100')): rule 3, a rung-5 move owed to G2. The hot-path oracle hashes copy output by nature, and commit 13c832a9f states the reason. One gap, filed above: the new rule-3 table test sets read_at by hand, so it encodes the ruling but not the production path.", "files": ["packages/evals/tests/test_number_policy_written_page.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_presentation_figure_forms.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_presentation_number_forms.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_report_charts.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_report_golden_g2.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_report_kit_contract.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_report_svg.py", "packages/evals/tests/test_writer_eval_preference.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "note", "title": "Outside the chunk: a borderline money gate reason joins its interval's ends with an en dash ('interval $5.10\u2013$5.80'), where rule 3 says every interval reads 'low to high'", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. report_gates.py:233 (`spread = f\", {words}{spelled(figure.ci[0])}\u2013{spelled(figure.ci[1])}\"`), from e85b4e488 in the earlier bundle and not touched by C5c-forms; it is rated only because the no-pre-existing-exception rule asks for it. Scenario: a cost criterion that reads borderline on its interval states 'Cost per 1,000 replies $5.41, interval $5.10\u2013$5.80', while the non-money branch and every other surface read '... to ...'. Join with ' to ', as `_spread` does through format_figure, in whichever chunk owns report_gates.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report_gates.py"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Q3: reads_as_share decides percent for the three pages, but the quick console and the writer eval's readings table still choose percent by other predicates, so one 0-1 measure reads 73% on the page and 0.73 in the CLI", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why: the chunk moved the page builders onto one predicate and left the CLI-side display decisions on the older ones. Inside the pages, reads_as_share (kernel/metrics.py:3173) is now the one descriptor-level decision. figures._is_share (figures.py:162-169) calls it for the aspect page. measure_kinds.rate_measures (measure_kinds.py:74-97) calls it for the scorecard and its forms (forms.py:495, scorecard.py:1691, 1775). report_input_build._unit (report_input_build.py:1056-1061) calls it once for both the summary path and the readings path, and stores the result as unit 'share', which figure_keys._measure_scale (figure_keys.py:673-687) and the look.quantity fmt (parts/look.py:472) only read. For a fresh input, those three cannot disagree. kit.js decides nothing: it prints lo_text/hi_text or formats by the fmt Python chose. figure_keys._rate_scale is a values heuristic for a stored scale, not a rate decision. Two display deciders remain outside it: (1) quick/result.py:552 and :601 pick percent by reads_as_rate, which by its own docstring excludes 'a numeric score that merely lies on 0 to 1'. (2) harness/writer_eval.py:687 picks it by `data_type == 'boolean' or pass_fail`. Scenario for (1): grader(relevance) returns a float with ranges={'relevance': (0, 1)}. quick/one_call.py:351/367 then keeps value_range (0,1) and declares no unit. The aspect page and the scorecard print 'Relevance 73%' (reads_as_share is True), while print(result) prints 'relevance: mean 0.73 (90 trials, min 0.1, max 1)' (_measured_text with rate=False, quick/result.py:1037-1038). Number rule 5 names the CLI among the renderers that print decided text. Scenario for (2): a writer host's unitless 0-1 measure that has a contrast reads '30%' through on_page/report_rulers (writer_eval.py:718-722), and one without a contrast reads '0.3' through forms.reading (writer_eval.py:724) in the same table. A minor third seam is the no-descriptor fallback, which differs by site: figures falls back to METRIC_DESCRIPTORS and then to classifier/goal-check names, measure_kinds to METRIC_DESCRIPTORS, and report_input_build to nothing. It is mostly unreachable, because bundle/surface._measure_catalog describes every name in the bundle. Construction: in the CLI and the writer eval, read the percent decision from the aspect page's own ruler (report_rulers(...).of(name, 'measure').unit == PERCENT) or from reads_as_share. Keep reads_as_rate only for the 'N of M trials' count, as the scorecard's counted_rates/rate_measures split already does. To bound the class, search `reads_as_rate(` callers that choose a printed form, and `data_type == \"boolean\"` display checks.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/quick/result.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/harness/writer_eval.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/kernel/metrics.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The unit-once rule (number rule 3) now has five hand-written owners, and charts.py re-decides it by stripping units off decided text and appending them again", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why: rule 3 was applied at each site that writes an interval, not issued once by forms. The sites: (1) forms._PrintedInterval.text/.digits/.beside (forms.py:243-254). (2) forms.glyph_title, with one branch per unit (forms.py:1898-1909). (3) forms._scale_interval (forms.py:3231-3249). (4) charts._unit_once, which now strips the unit from both ends (charts.py:549-561), plus _table_unit/_unit_after (charts.py:157-171), which append it again after the high end, after a value and after a reference, by sniffing `text.endswith(unit)` and 'contains a digit'. (5) writer_eval._preference_figure, which picks share_text over share_percent by hand (writer_eval.py:1839-1842). forms._decimals_in (forms.py:3123-3126) is the same move for digits: it reads a spread's precision back out of the mean's printed text by regex instead of taking the decided form's decimals. Plan section 3.1 / rule 5 says one module decides and every renderer prints decided text. Here the values-table builder rewrites decided text instead, and _restates (charts.py:622-636) has to guess which spellings count as the same figure. Consequence: the next ruling that moves a unit must be made in five places, and the residual list's item 5 ('the unit sits in three places') is the previous member of this class. Construction: have forms issue both spellings of every interval a chart carries, the one written alone (`.text`) and the one written beside its figure (`.beside`), and the value with its unit. The resolvers then put each on the row, and charts.table prints them without any suffix logic. ruled_spread takes the mean's decimals from the Printed the mean was issued as. Filing this as an issue is an acceptable disposition under the convergence rules. The fix is structural, and no current output is known to be wrong.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/charts.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/forms.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/harness/writer_eval.py"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "Q2: CV2's stored written page staying bare is acceptable under no-back-compat, but nothing on the page tells the reader, the input cannot (Measure has no value_range), and the Hosts line's date boundary undercounts what needs regenerating", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. What the stored page prints: report_input.Measure (report_input.py:444-470) carries unit, grade and label but no value_range. A stored measure with unit None and grade None therefore takes figure_keys._measure_scale -> 'plain' (figure_keys.py:673-687) in every written-page table. It reads 'Lost-call rate 0' with no '%', and its corrected comparison reads '-0.49 to +0.49' with no unit. No header, glossary line or caveat says these are fractions. A reader cannot infer the unit from value_range, because value_range appears neither on the page nor in the stored input. Inference rests on the word 'rate' and on figures lying in 0 to 1. The real misreading risk is '-0.49 to +0.49' taken as half a point, a hundredfold-narrow interval, when it is +/-49 pts. A page-side disclosure would mean detecting old inputs at render time, which is a shim, against the repo's 'no back compat' stance and the owner's 'a doc is often enough' bar. So no page change is recommended. Two changes are: (a) Make the CHANGELOG Hosts sentence an instruction (regenerate stored written reports whose aspects declare a unitless 0-1 measure), not a description. Before this chunk, the readings path in _Builder.measures (report_input_build.py:704-716) set no 'unit' at all, so an input generated after 2026-11-09 can also be bare for a measure that path first saw, and the date '(2026-11-09)' undercounts. (b) Re-shoot residual item 1 on CV2 only against a regenerated report, or it will read as still open.", "files": ["CHANGELOG.md", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report_input_build.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/figure_keys.py"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "Q4: the per-1,000 money ruler on aspect pages changes printed text and the in-memory table numbers only; no public name, field, stored or exported value moves", "recommendation": "Scope: none. This is a mandated answer and carries no defect. Evidence: in all four hot-path oracle fixtures, only the body:html, body:html_static and body:markdown hashes moved. Every body:json hash is unchanged. An aspect's JSON is its bundle (serialize.py:50-51), and the campaign JSON is code's readings, so nothing exported or stored changes. reads_as_share, counted_rates, MeasureRuler, reply_cost_ruler and PER_THOUSAND_REPLIES are absent from api/surface.json, and tests/fixtures/host_imports/discodon.txt names none of them. The Discodon imports in that area, kernel.METRIC_DESCRIPTORS and MetricDescriptor, are unchanged, so the surface-ceiling rule and the minor-bump growth rule are not engaged. The host-visible surfaces are: printed text in HTML, static SVG and Markdown (scoreboard head 'Cost per 1,000 replies', glossary term, cells, the scorecard bar's measure name), the quick console line, and, beyond the CHANGELOG's wording, the numeric table cells of an in-memory Report. build.py:1158 stores the scoreboard cost 'on the cost's ruler' as a number 'a program sorts by', so a host that reads CampaignReport aspect blocks programmatically now sees that number x1000. Discodon embeds report.html verbatim, so it is likely unaffected. One clause in the Hosts line ('a program reading an aspect page's table cells reads the cost per 1,000 replies') would close the gap. No code change is wanted.", "files": ["CHANGELOG.md", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/build.py"], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "figures.py and build.py still describe per-reply money as the default ruler with a per-1,000 fallback under REPLY_COST_FLOOR, and REPLY_COST_FLOOR is an exported constant that nothing reads", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why: _money_ruler now starts at 1,000 (figures.py:178), but the prose describing the old rule was kept. Stale claims: the figures.py module docstring (lines 10-12: 'A cost per reply under REPLY_COST_FLOOR on every arm is stated per 1,000 replies'), REPLY_COST_FLOOR's comment (51-52), measure_rulers' docstring (129-130), reply_cost_ruler's :return: (188-189: 'Money per reply, or per 1,000 ...'), and build._per_reply's docstring (1031), whose 'per reply' fallback now fires only with no bundle. REPLY_COST_FLOOR has no reader anywhere in src or tests, and it had none before this chunk. It is listed in figures.__all__ (line 266) but not in api/surface.json, so it can be removed safely. Remedy: delete REPLY_COST_FLOOR and its export, and delete the conditional clauses rather than rewording them, leaving the cost_unit.money_ruler cross-reference to say where the scale is chosen. The prose ceiling applies, and nothing reads these claims.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/figures.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/build.py"], "fid": "R-12"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "A compare chart's head now names one family's joint guarantee whenever any row is paired, though its unpaired rows (other places, independent spreads) are outside that family and carry their own level", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Genuinely ambiguous: whether a compare chart mixes rows in production is unverified. _family_level for source 'compare' (parts/resolve_changes.py:1263-1273) collects aspects only from rows with paired_with set, and pairing_contrast returns a contrast only for the overall place (figure_keys.py:4268). It then heads the whole chart with that family's guarantee ('95% intervals (adjusted for 10 comparisons)'). Each compare row carries its own level (figure_keys._Difference.level; the compare table states it per row in its 'level' column). A chart of overall plus strata would therefore head its stratum rows, whose intervals come from independent spreads, with a family adjustment they were never part of. The head is a level decision rebuilt from a filtered subset of rows. Make it relational: state the joint guarantee only where every interval-bearing row is of that family, and fall back to the per-row levels (or to context.level) otherwise. Also pin a mixed overall-plus-strata compare chart in a test.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/parts/resolve_changes.py"], "fid": "R-13"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The report input's code-readings path now writes each measure's unit, which moves the input's digest and snapshot id and changes how a readings-only measure reads on the written page, and the Hosts line says only that a stored snapshot reads a fraction bare", "recommendation": "Instance. report_input_build.py:704-716 (commit 58835d1b0) adds `\"unit\": _unit(described, grade)` to the facts a measure takes when it reaches the input through `bundle.readings.measures` before any cell summary does. Before the chunk that dict carried no unit, so every such measure entered `Measure.unit` as None. `Measure.unit` is part of `report_input_digest` (report_input.py:861, sha256 of the dumped model), so it is part of a snapshot's id. Scenario: a host such as Discodon regenerates a production campaign's report input after upgrading. One of its measures reaches the input only on that path: a host fraction, or a money or ms measure with a declared unit. Its unit moves from None to `share`, `usd` or `ms`, the digest moves, and a scheduled mint of that campaign's writer case is refused as moved evidence. The written page also starts reading a readings-only money measure in money form, where it read a bare number. The C5b entry (CHANGELOG.md:164-168) disclosed exactly this consequence for the `share` unit. This entry